      "extracellular": true,
      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface display confirmed by non-permeabilized anti-YFP antibody staining (L3). NanoBRET-based binding assay with BODIPY-cyclopamine confirmed functional ligand binding of Nluc-tagged SMO (L4/L5). Live-cell ELISA with anti-Nluc antibody confirmed surface expression (L7). Gi signaling activity confirmed for HA/Flag N-terminal constructs (L13).",
      "gene_symbol": "SMO",
      "insert_after_residue": 27,
      "library": null,
      "linker": null,
      "loop_run_position": null,
      "median_conservation": null,
      "numbered_against": null,
      "plddt": null,
      "position_evidence": "topology_derived",
      "position_frame": null,
      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "The signal peptide spans residues 1-27 (authoritative landmark); the mature N-terminus begins at residue 28 (R). A tag inserted C-terminal to the signal sequence therefore sits at insert_after_residue=27, placing it at the very start of the 204-residue extracellular domain, 204 residues from TM1. This is the only extracellular terminus of SMO and is confirmed by at least eight independent published constructs (L2, L3, L4, L5, L6, L7, L8, L10, L12, L13) using tags ranging from 11 aa (HiBiT) to ~240 aa (YFP), all surface-displayed and functional. The position is topology_derived because the papers describe the junction as 'C-terminal to the signal sequence' without citing a specific residue number; with a single extracellular terminus, only one junction is possible. Wild-type mouse Smo was tagged at the N terminus with YFP or the SNAP tag in the pCS2+ vector (full-length, membrane-anchored construct). The tag was placed C-terminal to the signal sequence. YFP-Smo cells showed surface-exposed Smo by non-permeabilized anti-YFP staining before and after Shh addition. Multiple independent studies (L4/L5/L6/L7: Nluc; L8: FLAG-SNAP; L10: FLAG; L12: YFP; L13: HA/Flag) all place tags at the same N-terminal extracellular junction.",
      "residue_after": "R",
